<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>The plaintiff sued for possession of the second and third floors of premises No. 404 (renumbered 404-B), Gali Matia Mahal, Jama Masjid, Delhi, together with damages for use and occupation. His title rested on a registered sale deed of 1989, whose recitals traced the vendor&rsquo;s ownership to a 1978 partition deed. The defendant — a family member — resisted on three fronts: a Hibanama (oral gift reduced to writing) of 1974 from his father; alternatively, ownership by adverse possession; and, in any event, the bar of Section 19 of the Slum Areas (Improvement and Clearance) Act, 1956. The trial court decreed the suit on 30 April 2026 with mesne profits of Rs 8,000 per month from November 2021, enhanced periodically. The defendant appealed under Section 96 CPC.</p>

<h2>How each defence fell</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The registered sale deed stood.</strong> A registered document carries a presumption of valid execution — the Court invoked Prem Singh v. Birbal, (2006) 5 SCC 353 — and the defendant produced no evidence of forgery. A missing annexed site plan was inconsequential where the deed&rsquo;s recitals, the plaintiff&rsquo;s site plan and the defendant&rsquo;s own description identified the property.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The Hiba had nothing to convey.</strong> Testing the 1974 Hibanama against Mohammedan law principles (Hafeeza Bibi v. Shaikh Farid, (2011) 5 SCC 654), the Court found the fatal defect upstream: the donor&rsquo;s own title was never proved.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Adverse possession lacked an animus.</strong> &ldquo;Mere length of possession, howsoever prolonged, does not confer ownership by adverse possession&rdquo; — occupation must be hostile, open and to the owner&rsquo;s knowledge for the statutory period. The defendant&rsquo;s occupation began as a licence from the plaintiff, and a licensee&rsquo;s possession is permissive by definition.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The slum-law shield belonged to tenants.</strong> Per the Full Bench in Punnu Ram, &ldquo;a mere occupier cannot be equated to a tenant&rdquo;; Section 19 protection does not extend to persons claiming independent title or holding as licensees.</div>
</div>

<h2>The licence findings</h2>
<p>The Court characterised the defendant&rsquo;s occupation as a licence granted by the plaintiff to a family member after he became owner — and made two practical observations that recur in Delhi possession litigation. First, a licence of this kind needs no writing and no date; family arrangements rarely generate paperwork, and their informality does not convert them into tenancies. Second, on termination, service of notice was established on the postal and tracking record — but even if service were disputed, the institution of the suit itself terminates a licence, a proposition supported by Nopany Investments v. Santokh Singh, (2008) 2 SCC 728. The defences, pulled together, were mutually destructive: a man cannot at once be owner by gift, owner by hostile possession, and a protected tenant.</p>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Defence</th><th>Essential requirement</th><th>Where it failed</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Hiba (gift)</td><td>Donor must hold title</td><td>Father&rsquo;s ownership never proved</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Adverse possession</td><td>Hostile assertion to owner&rsquo;s knowledge for the statutory period</td><td>Occupation was permissive — a family licence</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 19, Slum Areas Act</td><td>Status of tenant</td><td>Occupier claiming title is not a tenant</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Why the judgment matters</h2>
<p>Old-city properties in Delhi generate exactly this litigation pattern: long family occupation, informal arrangements, and defences assembled decades later from whatever documents survive. The judgment is a reminder that first appellate courts re-appreciate evidence but do not rescue incoherent defence structures — and that pleading adverse possession alongside a title claim invites the court to treat both with scepticism. For owners, it underlines the quiet strength of a registered sale deed with clean recitals; for occupants, it shows the cost of resting decades of possession on a gift whose giver had nothing to give.</p>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment for general information only and is not legal advice on any property dispute.</div>
